This is the same setup used in the sigmoid example earlier in the course notebook, except the activation function here is ReLU instead of sigmoid. Since ReLU just clips negative values to zero, most of the arithmetic below is the same weighted-sum-plus-bias formula from before, just passed through max(0, z) instead of the sigmoid curve.

The task specifies $f = max(0, Z_n)$, which is just ReLU. If the weighted sum is negative, the unit outputs 0; otherwise it passes the value through unchanged.

def relu(z):
    return max(0, z)

For the forward pass, only $f(z) = max(0, z)$ is needed, same as in Laboratory Task 2. But for the backward pass, the derivative of ReLU is also needed, since backpropagation relies on the chain rule.f'(z) = \begin{cases} 1 & z > 0 \\ 0 & z \le 0 \end{cases}

Basically, if a unit was "on" during the forward pass (output greater than 0), the gradient flows through it. If it was "off" (clipped to 0), no gradient flows through it at all.

def relu(z):
    return np.maximum(0, z)

def relu_deriv(z):
    return (z > 0).astype(float)

def display_image(batch, labels=None, n=8):
    batch = batch.detach().numpy()
    fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, n, figsize=(12, 2))
    for i in range(n):
        axs[i].imshow(batch[i, 0, :, :], cmap='gray')
        axs[i].axis('off')
        if labels is not None:
            axs[i].set_title(labels[i].item())
    plt.show()

